  ],
  "text": "Learn something today and the physical shape of your brain is different tonight. A memory is not a file written somewhere. There is no drive in there. It is a connection between cells, and connections get stronger with use. Neurons that fire together grow more contact points between them. Repeat it and that pathway physically thickens, like a track worn across grass. Which means a memory has to be built, and building takes time. Most of that building happens while you sleep, not while you study. So cramming loads all the input in and skips the construction entirely. Spacing it out lets the structure set between sessions. That is the whole reason it wins. You are not memorising. You are laying down something you can walk on later. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
